ABSTRACT
Efficient thermal management remains a critical challenge in aerospace propulsion systems, particularly in the thrust chambers of liquid rocket engines. Traditional regenerative cooling channels often fail to fully leverage intricate geometries, limiting their performance. Leveraging advanced 3D printing, this study proposes two TPMS-based conformal design strategies for regenerative cooling channels: topological conformal strategies, including gradient and linearity, as well as modular conformal strategies employing geometric unit division. Using computational fluid dynamics simulations validated by experimental results, we systematically assess the flow and heat transfer characteristics, cooling uniformity, and overall efficacy of the proposed designs. Results reveal that optimizing TPMS cell features by boolean operations is essential for enhancing channel performance, and the Diamond structure exhibiting minimal sensitivity to this operation. Among the designs, the modular conformal channel Sp-A, based on a spherical geometry, achieves superior performance. The Sp-A channel improved overall cooling performance by 46 %, with a 69 % enhancement in circumferential temperature uniformity and a 7 % reduction in specific pressure drop compared to traditional grooved channels. These findings highlight the significant potential of TPMS-based conformal cooling channels in high-performance regenerative cooling systems, and introduce a novel design paradigm for thermal management in complex aerospace geometries.
1. Induction
In the context of rapid advancements in global energy management and aerospace technology, enhancing the cooling efficiency of liquid rocket engine (LRE) thrust chambers remains a critical challenge in spacecraft design [1]. As the heart of a rocket engine, the thrust chamber endures extreme thermal loads and high pressures, making the performance of its cooling system essential to both the overall efficiency and the operational lifespan of the chamber [2]. Regenerative cooling channels have proven to be an effective solution for ensuring the long-term stability and functionality of thrust chambers in such extreme environments [3]. Fuel circulates along the chamber walls, absorbing heat from combustion, preheating, and lowering the temperature of the
chamber [4]. A key component of the thrust chamber, the Laval nozzle, features a convergent-divergent geometry that accelerates gas flow to supersonic speeds, significantly enhancing thrust [5]. However, the complex geometry of the Laval nozzle introduces significant challenges for designing efficient regenerative cooling channels, necessitating the development of adaptable cooling solutions for these intricate spaces essential to optimizing thrust chamber performance. Conventional cooling channel designs often rely on linear or simple geometric configurations, which frequently fail to provide ideal cooling performance within the complex geometry of thrust chambers [6]. Early studies primarily focused on optimizing straight-through channels fabricated using milling techniques, investigating structural parameters such as aspect ratios [7,8] and channel wall thickness [9]. To further
