e, minimizing flow resistance, and achieving uniform temperature distribution.
In response to these challenges, the use of triply periodic minimal surface (TPMS) structures in cooling channel design has gained increasing attention. TPMS refers to a class of three-dimensional surfaces that exhibit periodicity and minimal surface area [22,23]. With their high specific strength, excellent heat transfer properties, and energy absorption capabilities, TPMS structures have found wide applications in fields such as aerospace [24,25], biomedical materials [26], and heat exchangers [27,28]. Furthermore, the design flexibility of TPMS structures allows them to be tailored for diverse applications. Various optimization strategies have been employed to improve TPMS performance, including gradient design of geometric parameters [29–31], integration of heterogeneous structures [32,33], multi-scale feature incorporation [34,35], and topological deformation [36,37]. These strategies have led to significant improvements in mechanical properties, as well as in mass and heat transfer performance. However, research on TPMS structures, particularly in the context of heat and mass transfer has remained primarily focused on the design of TPMS unit cell parameters [38,39], or their direct application in heat exchangers [40, 41] and catalytic supports [42]. Yeranee et al. [43], employed topology optimization to develop high-performance serpentine cooling channels with Diamond TPMS structures, improving overall heat transfer while simultaneously reducing flow resistance. In addition, Zhang et al. [44] designed three radially graded TPMS porous structures in polar form, demonstrating the efficacy of radial density gradient design in regulating the thermal storage process of composite TPMS-phase change material (PCM) systems. Although TPMS structures have shown great potential in various applications, implementing them in complex engineering geometries presents several challenges. Conformal design, which precisely matches functional structures to the shape and curvature of a given surface or space, making it ideal for maximizing spatial utilization [45,46]. By
