integrating TPMS structures with conformal design, researchers aim to improve heat transfer performance and optimize structural performance in intricate geometries. However, most studies on conformal design have focused primarily on mechanical optimization using cartesian coordinate systems or volumetric mesh partitioning [47–49]. Their application in mass and heat transfer optimization need further investigation [50]. The main challenge lies in the difficulty of adapting TPMS cell-filling methods based on cartesian coordinate to flow spaces with complex surface features. Moreover, traditional meshing techniques present a dilemma: excessively dense meshes may lead to increased flow resistance, while sparse meshes fail to capture intricate curvature accurately. Therefore, effectively using TPMS structures in the complex cooling space of a thrust chamber, while balancing heat transfer performance, flow resistance, and temperature uniformity, remains a significant technical challenge.
In this study, we propose topological and modular conformal design strategies to develop a variety of TPMS-based conformal channels tailored for the complex cooling spaces of a thrust chamber, and exploring their potential applications in regenerative cooling systems. We systematically evaluate the flow and heat transfer characteristics, circumferential temperature uniformity, flow resistance, and overall performance of each channel through numerical simulations. The results reveal the enhancement mechanism through which TPMS conformal channels improve cooling performance compared to traditional grooved channel, and provide insights into how different design strategies influence channel performance. This research lays a theoretical foundation for designing efficient regenerative cooling systems in complex geometries and offers new perspectives for developing other highperformance cooling systems.
