2. Conformal design in complex spaces
2.1. Channel layout space
The thrust chamber of a liquid rocket engine (LRE) typically features a thin-walled cavity that houses regenerative cooling channels. The inlet and outlet ports for the cooling medium are positioned at the upper and lower sections of the thrust chamber, ensuring even coolant distribution through a collection chamber [51], with an inlet and outlet segment extending approximately 10 times the hydraulic diameter (Dh) to facilitate fully developed flow before entering the cooling channels, as shown in Fig. 1(a). To accommodate thermal gradients and pressure changes in different regions, the cooling channel layout is defined by two characteristic curves with uneven offsets along the Z-axis. These curves precisely control the shape and boundary of the free surfaces within the complex spatial geometry. This design mirrors the converging-diverging characteristics of a Laval nozzle, with Fig. 1(b) illustrating the relative offset between the inner and outer curves along the Z-direction. An expanded cooling space was created in the throat region of the thrust chamber (Z = 150 mm) to manage the extreme high-temperature and high-pressure conditions, improving both cooling efficiency and structural stability. The conformal design proposed in this study aims to
develop optimized channels with TPMS-based structures within this expanded space, thereby enhancing heat exchange efficiency and optimizing flow paths.
2.2. Topological conformal design
2.2.1. Gradient conformal mapping design
Given the geometric differences between the channel layout within the thrust chamber and typical TPMS structures, this study employs a gradient conformal mapping strategy to achieve precise topological deformation of TPMS unit cells, enabling them to conform to the target space. Fig. 2 illustrates the design process. First, an array of TPMS unit cells is generated to create the preliminary channel units. These units are then deformed topologically, allowing the cell boundaries to adaptively fit the 36◦ sector of the target space. Finally, the channel units are arranged circumferentially to form a complete conformal cooling channel. This methodology allows dynamic adjustment of TPMS structures to match the complexity of the surrounding geometry. It ensures adequate filling and structural integrity within the target space, without requiring complex boolean operations. This approach is termed gradient conformal (G-C) design. Based on this method, three representative TPMS unit cells (Schwarz, Gyroid, and Diamond) are selected to construct the S(G-C), G(G-C), and D(G-C) cooling channels, respectively. The specific surface area (SSA) and hydraulic diameter (Dh) of these unit cells, which are critical for evaluating their heat transfer and flow characteristics, are summarized in Table 1.
