2.3. Modular conformal design
In addition to topologically deforming TPMS unit cells, modular
arrangements of geometric cells combined with boolean operations offer another effective approach for conformal design in complex spaces. Fig. 4(a) illustrates three fundamental modular arrangements—spherical (Sp-A), rectangular (Re-A), and columnar (Cy-A)—as well as the unit partitioning used in the topological conformal design. Each arrangement partitions the geometry into multiple units, which are then filled with TPMS cells. These arrangements are tailored based on spatial characteristics along three partitioning directions (1, 2, 3), influencing the structural features of the channels. The Sp-A takes the center of the space as the origin, adjusting the number of cells in the longitudinal, latitudinal, and radial directions. The Re-A configuration modifies cell dimensions and quantities along the X, Y, and Z axes in a cartesian coordinate system, making it a commonly used conformal method. The Cy-A revolves around the Z-axis, controlling the number of partitions in the circumferential, axial, and radial directions. While the Cy-A shares similarities with topological conformal design, the key distinction lies in the latter’s reliance on complex spatial feature curves for axial unit partitioning. Notably, all conformal methods, except Re-A, include control in both the circumferential and radial directions. This circumferential control
