3. Numerical simulation
3.1. CFD method and calculation settings
Due to the high costs of experiments, numerical simulations are widely used for the design and optimization of regenerative cooling channels. For numerical model simplification, the following assumptions were made:
• Steady-state and time-independent fluid flow and heat transfer. • Incompressible fluid with density variations modeled via the Boussinesq approximation. • Constant thermal conductivity, dynamic viscosity, and specific heat capacity of the working fluid. • Negligible radiation heat transfer, with heat transfer dominated by conduction and convection. • Fully developed flow at the inlet and outlet sections.
This study employs ANSYS Fluent 2023 R1 [55], a commercial computational fluid dynamics software, to analyze the thrust chamber. The simulated thrust chamber features smooth transitional characteristics, using copper as the material and water as the working fluid [6]. To facilitate experimental comparisons, the inner wall of the shell is maintained at a constant temperature of 100 ◦C. The inlet and outlet sections, along with the collection chamber, are designed to ensure uniform flow development of the coolant. The boundary conditions defined as a velocity inlet and a pressure outlet for unrestricted flow. The simulation utilizes the Boussinesq approximation to account for variations in fluid density throughout the solution process. The flow and heat transfer analyses are based on the Navier-Stokes equations, employing a coupled scheme for pressure-velocity coupling. Pressure is discretized with a second-order method, while momentum and energy are discretized using a second order upwind scheme. The continuity equation is expressed as follows:
