reduction in heat transfer performance. Additionally, the tangential inlet velocity restricts the vertical expansion of the fluid within the channel, resulting in a pronounced circumferential velocity gradient. Low-velocity regions are prone to heat accumulation, and each groove channel is isolated, preventing lateral mixing and heat exchange, further diminishing convective heat transfer performance. In contrast, the porous structure of TPMS-based channels effectively promotes fluid mixing. Fig. 10(a) illustrates the velocity distribution and flow characteristics of three gradient conformal (G-C) channels. The integrity of the TPMS unit cells preserves their unique hydrodynamic properties, particularly the influence of the “through-hole” structure on velocity distribution [56]. The S(G-C) channel, based on the Schwarz unit cell, features five straight through-holes along the flow direction, allowing rapid fluid passage and generating secondary flows at the junctions of adjacent channels. This induces vortices, enhances flow disturbances, thins the thermal boundary layer, and significantly improves heat transfer. On the other hand, the Gyroid unit cell contains four through-flow channels, and its cashew-shaped structure creates multiple high-velocity primary flows and secondary flows deviating from the main stream in the G(G-C) channel. In the throat region where the cross-sectional area narrows, the fluid velocity increases significantly, further enhancing local heat exchange. The Diamond unit cell, with its through-holes oriented diagonally, directs the fluid in the D (G-C) channel along a spiral path, forming alternating high- and low-velocity regions. Vortices are generated in the low-velocity regions due to shear forces between fluid layers. Fig. 10(b) shows the temperature distribution of the fluid. The S(G-C) channel exhibits a larger low-temperature region due to its extensive high-velocity zones and vortex activity. The difference in outlet temperatures is primarily attributed to the residence time of the fluid, with the spiral flow path of the D(G-C) channel effectively extending the fluid’s residence time and enhancing heat exchange. The linear conformal (L-C) design reduces the structural integrity and geometric complexity of TPMS unit cells while expanding the flow passage. As shown in Fig. 11, the widened through-channels in the S(L
C) channel result in reduced flow velocities, leading to an increase in the outlet temperature. The Gyroid unit cell features alternating wavy and cashew-shaped cross-sections. After trimming the unit cell, the G (L-C) flow channel structure exhibits circumferential wavy and cashewshaped patterns. This forces the upward-flowing fluid into periodic lateral motion, significantly enhancing fluid mixing. The abrupt directional changes generate localized velocity gradients, inducing vortex formation in low-velocity regions, which disrupts the thermal boundary layer and improves heat transfer. Conversely, the Diamond unit cell maintains a consistent cross-sectional profile, allowing the D(L-C) channel to retain a helical flow path with gentler velocity gradients. The simplified geometric structure reduces wall obstructions, facilitating easier fluid flow and acceleration. However, despite the overall increase in flow velocity, the reduction in high-intensity vortices driven by sharp velocity differentials, partially offsetting the potential heat transfer enhancement from increased flow rates. Fig. 12 illustrates the flow and heat transfer characteristics of modular conformal channels. The spherical modular design based on Diamond unit cells enables the Sp-A channel to exhibit distinct flow behaviors across different regions. The fluid initially passes through the Z-shaped channel at the lower expansion section, where continuous changes in flow direction extend the flow path and enhance fluid mixing. In the upper expansion section, the straight corrugated flow path forms a high-velocity mainstream in the central region, while the continuous curvature variations in the corrugations induce flow separation at the troughs, creating low-velocity reflux zones. Within these zones, reversed local flow generates vortices that significantly enhance heat transfer by intensifying turbulent mixing, leading to an elevated fluid temperatures in these regions. In the throat and contractio