5. Conclusions
In summary, this study developed and evaluated a range of TPMSbased conformal channels specifically designed for the complex internal layout of rocket thrust chamber cooling systems. Their performance was compared to that of traditional grooved channel. Using numerical simulations, we analyzed the thermo-hydraulic characteristics of each channel at various inlet velocities, with a comprehensive assessment of flow and heat transfer properties, cooling uniformity, and energy loss, particularly at the highest inlet velocity of 5 m/s. The main conclusions are as follows:
(1) The gradient and linear topological conformal designs demonstrate that the incompleteness of the TPMS cell is beneficial. This approach expands the flow passage, mitigates flow separation and shear effects caused by narrow channels, and thereby prevents significant degradation of convective heat transfer performance. Furthermore, the study reveals an intrinsic relationship between the continuity of unit cell cross-sections and their sensitivity to Boolean operations: Diamond unit cells, due to their smooth cross-sectional variations, are less prone to localized flow path constrictions after boolean operations, maintaining a uniform pressure gradient. (2) Although topological conformal designs can adapt to complex geometric spaces, the sharp curvature variations in the throat region of the thrust chamber cause excessive deformation of unit cells. While this enhances local TKE and improves heat transfer performance, it also leads to substantial flow energy dissipation. In contrast, modular conformal designs effectively suppress turbulence intensity through geometric unit partitioning. However, the absence of circumferential unit control in the base geometry (e.g., the planar symmetry of the Re-A channel) results in deteriorated cooling uniformity. Additionally, axial structural diversity (e.g., the Z-shaped-corrugated dual-mode structure of the
Sp-A channel) significantly enhances heat transfer performance by inducing localized velocity gradients and vortices. (3) The Sp-A channel demonstrated exceptional performance under high inlet velocities. At the maximum tested velocity of 5 m/s, the Sp-A channel outperformed traditional grooved channel, with a 46 % increase in overall performance, a 69 % improvement in temperature uniformity, and a 7 % reduction in specific pressure drop. These results indicate that modular conformal designs provide substantial advantages in terms of overall thermohydraulic efficiency.
In this study, we proposed new design strategies for the regenerative cooling system in rocket thrust chambers, showcasing the potential of TPMS conformal designs in optimizing flow and heat transfer performance within complex cooling spaces. Additionally, it offers critical theoretical support and design insights for the application of TPMS structures in other high-performance cooling systems.
